WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.100 --> 00:00:01.220
How do we turn a

2
00:00:01.220 --> 00:00:02.880
new recurrence into a dynamic

3
00:00:02.880 --> 00:00:04.960
program? We will use the

4
00:00:04.960 --> 00:00:07.540
review's Virahanka definition, build its

5
00:00:07.540 --> 00:00:09.380
state graph and table, and

6
00:00:09.380 --> 00:00:11.920
derive memoised and bottom-up solutions

7
00:00:11.920 --> 00:00:13.240
from the same dependencies.

8
00:00:13.820 --> 00:00:16.300
Use the definition actually supplied

9
00:00:16.300 --> 00:00:18.480
in this course: both base

10
00:00:18.480 --> 00:00:20.680
values are one. For a

11
00:00:20.680 --> 00:00:22.880
larger index, add the value

12
00:00:22.880 --> 00:00:24.460
at the previous index to

13
00:00:24.460 --> 00:00:25.500
the value at the floor

14
00:00:25.500 --> 00:00:27.340
of half the index; do

15
00:00:27.340 --> 00:00:29.080
not replace this recurrence with

16
00:00:29.080 --> 00:00:30.860
a familiar Fibonacci formula.

17
00:00:31.700 --> 00:00:33.100
Let the state V of

18
00:00:33.100 --> 00:00:35.220
j mean exactly the value

19
00:00:35.220 --> 00:00:36.760
of that recurrence at index

20
00:00:36.760 --> 00:00:39.400
j. Both dependencies are smaller

21
00:00:39.400 --> 00:00:40.840
than j when j exceeds

22
00:00:40.840 --> 00:00:43.700
one, which permits an increasing-index

23
00:00:43.700 --> 00:00:44.440
table order.

24
00:00:44.860 --> 00:00:46.340
Begin the table with one

25
00:00:46.340 --> 00:00:47.460
at zero and one at

26
00:00:47.460 --> 00:00:48.980
one. For a request at

27
00:00:48.980 --> 00:00:51.340
zero, return its base value

28
00:00:51.340 --> 00:00:52.800
before attempting to write index

29
00:00:52.800 --> 00:00:54.060
one in an array that

30
00:00:54.060 --> 00:00:55.360
may contain only a single

31
00:00:55.360 --> 00:00:55.760
slot.

32
00:00:56.340 --> 00:00:58.240
At index two, the dependencies

33
00:00:58.240 --> 00:01:00.060
are index one and floor

34
00:01:00.060 --> 00:01:01.800
of two over two, which

35
00:01:01.800 --> 00:01:03.820
is also one. Add the

36
00:01:03.820 --> 00:01:05.600
stored one twice, giving two;

37
00:01:05.600 --> 00:01:07.320
repeated dependence on a state

38
00:01:07.320 --> 00:01:09.080
does not require computing that

39
00:01:09.080 --> 00:01:09.840
state twice.

40
00:01:10.300 --> 00:01:12.780
At index three, the dependencies

41
00:01:12.780 --> 00:01:14.960
are index two and index

42
00:01:14.960 --> 00:01:17.320
one, giving two plus one

43
00:01:17.320 --> 00:01:19.300
equal to three. At index

44
00:01:19.300 --> 00:01:22.080
four, use index three and

45
00:01:22.080 --> 00:01:24.340
index two, giving three plus

46
00:01:24.340 --> 00:01:25.740
two equal to five.

47
00:01:26.100 --> 00:01:27.940
At index five, use V

48
00:01:27.940 --> 00:01:29.080
of four plus V of

49
00:01:29.080 --> 00:01:30.620
two to get seven. At

50
00:01:30.620 --> 00:01:32.220
index six, use V of

51
00:01:32.220 --> 00:01:33.300
five plus V of three

52
00:01:33.300 --> 00:01:34.940
to get ten, completing the

53
00:01:34.940 --> 00:01:36.980
table one, one, two, three,

54
00:01:37.200 --> 00:01:39.060
five, seven, ten.

55
00:01:39.260 --> 00:01:41.480
In a bottom-up implementation, loop

56
00:01:41.480 --> 00:01:42.560
j from two to n

57
00:01:42.560 --> 00:01:45.100
inclusive. Each iteration reads the

58
00:01:45.100 --> 00:01:46.960
two required earlier cells and

59
00:01:46.960 --> 00:01:48.180
stores their sum in V

60
00:01:48.180 --> 00:01:49.820
at j, then the function

61
00:01:49.820 --> 00:01:50.980
returns V at n.

62
00:01:51.300 --> 00:01:54.060
A top-down implementation instead checks

63
00:01:54.060 --> 00:01:55.560
whether V at the requested

64
00:01:55.560 --> 00:01:57.720
index is already cached. If

65
00:01:57.720 --> 00:01:59.900
not, it recursively obtains those

66
00:01:59.900 --> 00:02:02.120
same two smaller states, adds

67
00:02:02.120 --> 00:02:03.400
them and stores the result

68
00:02:03.400 --> 00:02:04.440
before returning.

69
00:02:05.260 --> 00:02:06.800
There are n plus one

70
00:02:06.800 --> 00:02:08.880
state positions, and each costs

71
00:02:08.880 --> 00:02:10.540
a constant number of arithmetic

72
00:02:10.540 --> 00:02:13.360
and cache operations. Both implementations

73
00:02:13.360 --> 00:02:15.460
therefore take linear arithmetic time

74
00:02:15.460 --> 00:02:17.980
and linear table space; the

75
00:02:17.980 --> 00:02:20.140
recursive version additionally counts its

76
00:02:20.140 --> 00:02:21.800
possible linear stack depth.

77
00:02:22.260 --> 00:02:24.320
Two rolling values are not

78
00:02:24.320 --> 00:02:26.360
sufficient here, because a future

79
00:02:26.360 --> 00:02:27.900
state may need an older

80
00:02:27.900 --> 00:02:30.660
half-index entry. The dependency pattern

81
00:02:30.660 --> 00:02:32.000
decides what memory can be

82
00:02:32.000 --> 00:02:34.700
discarded, so a Fibonacci optimisation

83
00:02:34.700 --> 00:02:36.740
cannot be copied just because

84
00:02:36.740 --> 00:02:38.720
both definitions contain two terms.

85
00:02:39.340 --> 00:02:41.000
Things to take note of:

86
00:02:41.420 --> 00:02:43.220
preserve the supplied bases and

87
00:02:43.220 --> 00:02:45.460
floor operation, justify the fill

88
00:02:45.460 --> 00:02:47.480
order, and distinguish repeated use

89
00:02:47.480 --> 00:02:50.160
from repeated computation. The method

90
00:02:50.160 --> 00:02:52.760
is state, dependencies, order and

91
00:02:52.760 --> 00:02:55.220
cost; remembering someone else's loop

92
00:02:55.220 --> 00:02:56.540
without those meanings is a

93
00:02:56.540 --> 00:02:58.120
rather fragile sort of confidence.
